Complete these steps in order before publishing a product collection.

1. Check metadata, licensing, dependencies, runtime floors and author handles.
2. Replace the invented existing-only fixture with a researched product
   resource. Keep the invariant files in `DEVELOPMENT.md` unchanged and adapt
   the typed declarations in `contract_subject.py` with real information entry
   points and a fake adapter. Declare mutation granularity in the adapter's
   `Contract`: `fieldwise` is the default; use `atomic` for an indivisible
   write. Keep the complete effect, uncertainty and no-redispatch checks for
   either declaration.
3. Declare `Contract.write_command` with the native write token sequences.
   The default matches `update --`; the alternate fixture matches `set --`.
   Keep the real subprocess-start injection and unchanged/no-dispatch checks.
4. Declare accepted arguments, their literal argv elements and refused
   arguments in `Contract.literals`. The default accepts a shell-shaped label
   and refuses an overlong one. Closed vocabularies declare their real values.
   Keep pre-dispatch validation and the separate executable transport cases.
5. Implement `inject_host_identity()` by changing the prepared host identity.
   The fixture substitutes a mismatched numeric UID. Keep the public locator
   valid and retain the separate tests for unsupported locator options.
6. Declare `Contract.environment_policy`: `refuse` by default, or
   `neutralize` with observed removal or empty values before child start.
   Document inherited, module-level and target environments. Preserve explicit
   locale and secret-value checks for both policies.
7. Declare each native fault in `Contract.faults` independently. Defaults
   reconcile matching effects and recognize a protocol-proven `refused` exit.
   A `NativeFault` may retain an effect or an uncertain rejection, with its own
   stable error and optional pending code. Retain effect evidence,
   redaction, no redispatch, actual deadlines and transport nondispatch.
8. Declare `expected` and `literal_expected` using `Expectations`: exact
   managed state, complete public projection, before/after diff and changed
   field names. The default fixture is flat; the second configuration preserves
   nested values and native origins. Fieldwise writes also declare exact
   partial state. Keep preservation and every readback assertion exact.
9. Keep observational subjects free of writers. Register a first mutator with
   its change, repeat, prediction and uncertainty cases in the same change.
   Creation and deletion require executable fixtures that exercise every new
   case.
10. Qualify the product's locator, including application identity, effective
    environment, one shared deadline and descendant cleanup. The example
    supports a trusted foreground program only.
11. State the native identity and concurrency guarantees. The fixture's
    conditional update under a file lock supplies no guarantee for a different
    product.
12. Set the compatibility series and exclusions from tested builds. Select the
    binding's compatibility policy and document it.
13. The engine remains shared for products with qualified definitions. This
    variant has no definition-driven CLI example. Its resource registry binds
    `FIXTURE` to the hook class; replace that registration with the product's
    resources.
14. The HTTP inventory extractor is dropped. Add a CLI inventory extractor only
    when a versioned product interface supplies the source, and retain source
    revision and extraction hashes beside its development output.
15. Adapt the real executable, seeded integration targets, process-fault inputs
    and managed-host scenario together. Keep the standalone module-process case
    crossing the actual utils subprocess boundary.
16. Update the README's execution, ownership, compatibility and usage
    statements.
    Replace its fixture qualification with the product builds and locators
    tested. Run the checks in [DEVELOPMENT.md](./DEVELOPMENT.md), then remove
    this checklist.
